{:edb/db-before-t 2778 :edb/db-after-t 2779 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536091 50 #inst "2026-10-09T14:46:13.760Z" 13194139536091 true] [17592186179603 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383189 13194139536091 true] [17592186179603 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383203 13194139536091 true] [17592186179604 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383196 13194139536091 true] [17592186179658 1020 #edb/ref 17592186383210 13194139536091 true] [17592186179940 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536091 true] [17592186179940 1100 "If the path $C$ is the line segment $MN^(―)$ traversed from the point $M(-1, 0)$ to the point $N(2, 4)$, then $∫_(C)ydx - xdy =$" 13194139536091 false] [17592186179940 1100 "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{MN}$ traversed from the point $M(-1,0)$ to the point $N(2,4),$ then ${\\int }_{C}y\\,dx-x\\,dy=$" 13194139536091 true] [17592186179940 1145 "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i$ and $b=2\\,i+4\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =-i+t(2\\,i+4\\,j-(-i)) \\\\  & =-i+t(3\\,i+4\\,j) \\\\  & =(3t-1)\\,i+4t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=3t-1$ and $y(t)=4t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=3\\,i+4\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx-x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4t(3)-(3t-1)(4)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4\\,dt \\\\  & =4t\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4(1-0) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536091 true] [17592186383189 1017 #edb/ref 17592186383190 13194139536091 true] [17592186383189 1089 "q-124651" 13194139536091 true] [17592186383189 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536091 true] [17592186383189 1100 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,dx+xy\\,dy,$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=4$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(2,0)$ to the point $B(0,1).$" 13194139536091 true] [17592186383189 1113 #uuid "35a5240c-df73-5f77-b045-605d4d50b078" 13194139536091 true] [17592186383189 1145 "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2$ and $1,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,dx+xy\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+xy\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}({x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+xy\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-8{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536091 true] [17592186383190 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383191 13194139536091 true] [17592186383190 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383192 13194139536091 true] [17592186383190 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383193 13194139536091 true] [17592186383190 1000 #edb/ref 17592186383194 13194139536091 true] [17592186383190 1000 #edb/ref 175921